content.text:
I read the three files before answering.

```src/http/client.ts``` is 61 lines: a ```request<T>()``` that takes method / path / body, calls fetch with a 10s AbortSignal timeout, and JSON-parses the response. No error handling at all. ```src/orders/fulfillmentClient.ts``` calls it three times in sequence from ```placeOrder()```. ```src/orders/reserveStock.ts``` is where the ECONNRESET actually surfaces, and it is the first of the three, which is why the log is full of "order not placed" rather than "label failed".

Plan: new file ```src/http/retry.ts``` exporting ```withRetry(fn, opts)``` plus a RetryOptions type. Every call site keeps calling ```request()``` and we wrap it once inside ```client.ts```, so nothing else has to change. That is the entire reason for putting it in the client rather than at the three call sites: a fourth call site added next quarter gets retries for free, and the person adding it never has to remember.

Shape I am going with:

```ts
export interface RetryOptions {
  attempts?: number;      // default 3
  baseDelayMs?: number;   // default 200
  maxDelayMs?: number;    // default 5_000
  idempotencyKey?: string;
  retryOn?: (err: unknown, attempt: number) => boolean;
  onRetry?: (info: RetryInfo) => void;
  sleep?: (ms: number) => Promise<void>;
  random?: () => number;
}

export async function withRetry<T>(
  fn: (attempt: number) => Promise<T>,
  opts: RetryOptions = {},
): Promise<T> { /* ... */ }
```

Delay is exponential — min(maxDelayMs, baseDelayMs * 2 ** (attempt - 1)) — with full jitter, so the actual sleep is random() times that number. I want to flag the tradeoff now rather than after it ships: full jitter gives up the predictable worst-case latency bound that fixed exponential backoff gives you, but it is the only variant that actually breaks up a thundering herd when forty Node processes all lose the same gateway in the same second. Deterministic backoff would re-synchronise us into the same wave, just later.

One thing I do not want to do: call the real setTimeout inside the wrapper. I am injecting sleep and random so the tests can run without real timers. Say the word if you would rather have it blocking.

Agreed, the functional form does not scale past two call sites. Refactoring to a class — but I am keeping withRetry exported so nothing outside src/http/ has to change yet. The payments service has its own copy of this logic and I am not breaking it in this PR; a follow-up can migrate it once this has survived a week.

```ts
// src/http/resilientClient.ts
export class ResilientClient {
  constructor(
    private readonly base: HttpClient,
    private readonly opts: Required<RetryOptions> & { breaker: CircuitBreaker },
  ) {}

  async request<T>(req: RequestSpec): Promise<T> {
    return this.breaker.guard(() =>
      withRetry(
        (attempt) => this.base.request<T>(req, {
          idempotencyKey: req.idempotencyKey ?? this.keyFor(req),
          signal: req.signal,
          attempt,
        }),
        { ...this.opts, onRetry: this.opts.onRetry ?? this.logRetry },
      ),
    );
  }

  // 409 means the warehouse already holds a reservation under this key.
  // Converge on the existing record instead of failing the order.
  private async onConflict<T>(req: RequestSpec): Promise<T> {
    const existing = await this.base.request<Reservation>({ method: 'GET', path: req.path + '/' + req.idempotencyKey });
    if (!existing) throw new ReservationConflictError(req.idempotencyKey);
    return existing as T;
  }
}

export function createResilientClient(base: HttpClient, opts?: Partial<ResilientOptions>): ResilientClient {
  return new ResilientClient(base, { attempts: 3, baseDelayMs: 200, maxDelayMs: 5_000, breaker: new CircuitBreaker({ threshold: 5, cooldownMs: 30_000, maxCooldownMs: 60_000 }), ...opts });
}
```

```fulfillmentClient.ts``` is now what you asked for:

```ts
this.http = createResilientClient(new HttpClient({ timeoutMs: 10_000 }));
await this.http.request({ method: 'POST', path: '/reservations', idempotencyKey: order.id, body: payload });
```

placeOrder() keeps calling this.http.request and knows about none of the retry, jitter, idempotency or breaker machinery. The 409 handling from two turns ago lives inside the class rather than at the call site, because a call site that forgets it is a double reservation, and the only thing worse than no 409 handling is 409 handling that exists in one of three places.

Tradeoff: a class is mutable state and is harder to share safely, so I am making ownership explicit — one instance per FulfillmentClient, constructed once at startup in ```src/index.ts```. I am deliberately not making it a module-level singleton, even though that would be the obvious way to share the breaker, because a breaker shared across unrelated callers lets one route's failures open the circuit for every other route. That is a much harder incident to reason about than the stampede we started with.
